you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  scientists say they've created the  whitest ever paint  which could help cool buildings and  tackle the climate crisis  more smiles than substance we've got the  latest from brussels  on a virtual meeting between the chinese  french and german leaders  all about climate change and saving the  octopus teacher  kelp forests one of the world's richest  ecosystems  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions and we aim to take you to the  heart of the climate crisis  explain the data driving the changes  that are already affecting us but also  show you  just how far we've come coming up more  on what we learned from today's meeting  between leaders from china  france and germany on climate issues but  first  scientists have developed what they  claim is the whitest ever paint  as a way of tackling global warming  engineers at purdue university in  indiana  say it could be used on the roofs of  buildings as a way of cooling them  reducing the need for air conditioning  well in a moment i'll be speaking to one  of the professors behind it but first  here's why it's important and how it  could make a difference  [Music]  it's it is better than current white  paint because it's reflecting  a lot more than conventional white paint  does  and as a result of it reflecting a lot  more a lot less heat is entering the  building  and therefore the demand for cooling  that building  is significantly reduced  [Music]  when white paint was put on the top of  this hospital the  patients who suffered from extreme heat  particularly on the top floor of that  hospital  were dramatically benefited so some  studies recommend  that using smart surfaces across cities  can reduce  their global warming impact by 10 to 15  percent and  reduce temperatures by about five degree  fahrenheit by mid-century  well joining me now from indiana is  professor shulin ruan one of the  engineers who developed the paint  welcome to you so how can an ultra white  paint  help tackle global warming so the  commercial  pain available on the market today they  only reflect  80 to 90 percent of sunlight which means  they absorb 10 to 20 percent sunlight  uh so different er pain we reflect  as high as 98.1 percent of sunlight back  to the space  so it only absorbs 1.9 percent of  sunlight  that's kind of a cutting the absorption  of p from the sun by 5 to 10 times so uh  you know that kind of really helps stop  the warming trend of the earth  and so how much whiter is this paint  than the current  current whitish shade and how much  difference do you think that will  actually make  our paint is you know looks much wider  than the other  commercial white paints available now  compared to the  commercial paint that reflects to about  80 percent  uh if you cover our paint on a house  with a 100  square meter floor size it can help  um you know uh reduce almost 20 kilo  20 kilowatt of the heating from the sun  so that's really  you know a big deal in some areas using  our paint on the roof can even totally  remove the need to turn on the air  conditioner how widely would you expect  modern buildings to use this how widely  used could it be  actually we already got a lot of  inquiries from the hot climates over the  world  such as india middle east africa  south america central america and then  south part of the united states  people already like painting their  houses with white  but again our white is much wider than  those they can purchase so hopefully  our paint can be available uh you know  uh  you know a year or two so people can use  it  now let's take a look at some of the  day's other climate news now and  scientists are warning that coral reefs  around hawaii  could be destroyed by heavy flooding  when storms occur  sediment is washed into the ocean and  onto the reefs  it blocks sunlight from hitting the  reefs making it harder for the coral to  get nutrients through  photosynthesis hawaii's state  climatologist says extreme rainfall will  become more common on the islands  as a result of global warming  a new hybrid of bear has been discovered  which could be more resilient  to climate change grizzly bears have  been mating with endangered polar bears  to create the so-called  pisley bear which experts say is better  adapted to warm weather  grizzly bears in alaska and canada have  been migrating north  as temperatures increase bringing them  into close contact with polar bears  and winemakers in france are hitting the  bottle after a difficult start to the  growing season  it's estimated at least a third of  french wines worth  almost 2 billion euros in sales will be  most lost this year due to unseasonably  cold temperatures in spring  government ministers say the extreme  weather is the result of climate change  now it's been a big week in climate  diplomacy so let's remind you of what's  been going on  and u.s climate envoy john kerry has  arrived in south korea  after several days of talks with  officials in china  meanwhile china's president xi jinping  has taken part in  a virtual climate change meeting with  the german chancellor angela merkel and  the french president  emmanuel macron well later today  president biden will host japan's prime  minister at the white house with issues  on climate change thought to be high on  the agenda  and the u.s president has invited world  leaders to a virtual earth day summit  next thursday russia and china have both  yet to confirm  if they're going to attend well there  were some positive  messages today following that meeting  between chinese french and german  leaders  so joining me now from brussels is our  correspondent michelle clifford so  michelle what did we learn from this  meeting today  well it was certainly very important to  get uh the chinese president uh on board  china is the world's biggest polluter  in terms of co2 emissions so angela  merkel  and president uh macron were very  pleased to have  uh president xi in the conversation what  we're being told  is that he reaffirmed china's goal of  co2  neutrality by 2060. that's a  a big deal in terms of a commitment and  he also decided to  sign up to what's known as the montreal  uh protocol kigali amendment to  strengthen  control of greenhouse gases  and the french and the germans said that  they were very pleased about that  uh indeed so um president xi uh  committed to working uh further with  germany and france and other  eu nations in terms of um issues of  climate change and of course the eu  at the moment has made very ambitious uh  climate uh  goals uh of its own committing to  reducing  greenhouse gas emissions uh by at least  55 percent by 2030  and aiming for climate neutrality uh by  2050 anna  thanks michelle the bafta award-winning  netflix documentary my octopus teacher  has become a cult hit  taking viewers into a world few have  ever seen  it focuses on filmmaker craig foster and  the wild octopus he follows for most of  her life  it's filmed in a kelp forest off the  coast of cape town in south africa  and now a campaign has been launched to  preserve the underwater ecosystem that's  under threat  as the world's oceans warm well earlier  i spoke to pippa erlich who directed the  documentary  and started by asking her about the  importance of kelp forests  [Applause]  hellfires are important because they are  very important carbons to  sequesters they provide habitat  to a very very rich uh  level of biodiversity and an incredible  community of species  they're also very productive ecosystems  and another thing that we're realizing  especially in the age of rising sea  levels is that kelp forests protect our  coastlines from storms and swells  but they're very vulnerable the ipcc  report which came out a couple of years  ago listed kelp forests as the second  most  vulnerable eco systems marine ecosystems  to climate change  and they're just beautiful magical  places you go  underwater in that kelp forest and it  feels like you're in  a wonderland and you put this  extraordinary relationship between craig  foster and the octopus at the heart of  your film it's quite  an emotional relationship was that a  deliberate decision  as soon as we saw the footage and we  heard how craig  described his experience the nature of  the story is that it's an emotional one  um it it it wasn't really deliberate it  was just  the story that we had in front of us and  we  we were very lucky to have something  that you know  could move people how do you move on  from such an  intense experience like that being able  to work on a film like this and with a  story like this  is truly a once in a lifetime experience  i work with the sea change project which  is the organization that  that craig co-founded almost 10 years  ago  and what we're trying to do is raise  awareness around kelp forests all over  the world  and specifically protect the great  african sea forest which is the far  the kelp forest where the octopus lived  and we have a lot of work to do and  we're starting with a big  local screening campaign later this year  and we've got a lot of other  exciting projects in the works the day  after the form launched we were getting  an email every three seconds and that  went on for weeks  we've received emails from psychologists  telling us that they're recommending  their film to their clients we've  received emails from young marine  biologists who  you know were really disillusioned in  the time of covert  about the careers that they were  starting out in and have decided to  carry on because they found the form  inspiring so  as a filmmaker and a storyteller to get  that kind of reaction to your work  is the most fulfilling thing that that  can happen the philippines is one of the  countries most at risk from the climate  crisis  because of sea level rises extreme  weather events  rising temperatures and severe rainfall  in the latest of our series of climate  diaries  youth activists mitzi janelle tan tells  us how her personal experiences of the  climate emergency  led to her campaigning to raise  awareness of climate issues  both in the philippines and around the  world  i grew up seeing the impacts of climate  change firsthand  having to be afraid of drowning in my  own bedroom  and sometimes waking up in the middle of  the night with flood water in my room  and having to scoop it out  cold and dark and afraid  growing up i would go to strangers and  talk to them about  greenhouse gas emissions and global  warming in the ozone layer  because climate education in the  philippines is  so lacking i didn't even make the  connection between the typhoons i was  already experiencing and the  greenhouse gas emissions i was talking  about we needed  an organization that specifically talked  about the climate and so  me and my friends we decided to put up  youth advocates for climate action  philippines  i realized that i have no choice i have  to join the struggle of our  environmental defenders and of the  people for  a better planet a better future a safe  presence  i had to join the fight  that's it for today's show but you can  get your climate fix over the weekend  with our podcast  on this week's episode find out how a  little golden toad  led to the world's most important  climate treaty that's on climatecast and  you can find it wherever you normally  get your podcast from  and of course you can watch our weekly  digital show with ali fortescue on  sky new social channels our app and  website thanks for watching  and see you next week

